   "l": "One who plants well is not pulled up,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Plant well, and it is not torn out;",
   "n": "者 makes a noun of whatever stands before it — 'the one who ——', the construction you met in chapter 49's 信者 and chapter 50's 善攝生者.\n\nWhat the noun refers to the Chinese does not settle. 善建者 is either the planter or the thing planted, and both give sense: the man who builds well is not dislodged, or what is well set in the ground is not torn out. Wang Bi takes the planter — 'he makes the root firm before he tends the branches, and so it is not pulled up' — and the literal above follows him; the idiomatic keeps the proverb, which works either way.",
   "p": true
  },
