   "l": "and sons and grandsons, by it, do not break off the sacrifices.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and his descendants keep up the offerings without a break.",
   "n": "To a reader of the fourth century BC this is not a pious flourish but the plain measure of a house that lasts. The dead were fed by their descendants; a line that failed was a line whose offerings stopped. 'The sacrifices do not break off' is the concrete way of saying that what was planted held.\n\n以 has nothing after it to govern: 祭祀 is what does not break off, not the means by which the descendants do something else. Wang Bi supplies the missing object — 子孫傳此道，以祭祀則不輟, 'sons and grandsons who hand this Way on will not break off in sacrificing'."
  },
