   ],
   "l": "Cultivate it in the district —\nits potency then long-lasting;\ncultivate it in the state —\nits potency then abundant;\ncultivate it in all under heaven —\nits potency then everywhere.\nTherefore: by a person observe a person,\nby a household observe a household,\nby a district observe a district,\nby a state observe a state,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Cultivate it in the district, and its potency lasts; cultivate it in the state, and its potency is abundant; cultivate it in all under heaven, and its potency is everywhere. So: look at a person by way of a person, at a household by way of a household, at a district by way of a district, at a state by way of a state,",
   "n": "以 X 觀 X puts the same noun on both sides of the verb, five times over, and that is the whole argument: you know another man by what you find in yourself, another household by your own. Wang Bi's comment is three characters — 彼皆然也, 'they are all like this' — which is as much as the line needs.\n\n長 is cháng here, 'long-lasting'. Three chapters ago, in 長之育之, the same graph was zhǎng, 'to bring on to growth'. Nothing marks the difference.\n\n鄉 is the district or neighbourhood, a real administrative rung between the household and the state, not a vague 'community'."
  },
